#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Add the Switch 2 games in ~/Games/Switch2 to Steam as Lichii shortcuts.

The shortcuts start /usr/bin/lichii-bc250 (the BC-250 fork) when it is
installed, else /usr/bin/lichii (the lichii package); --emulator picks one
(lichii, lichii-bc250, or the path of another Lichii, for example an AppImage).

The BC-250's driver offers mesh shaders (DirectMesh) that Lichii's mesh draws,
Zelda: Ocarina of Time's among them, do not run on yet: shortcuts of lichii get
EDEN_NX2_FORCE_MESH_FALLBACK=1, so those draws take Lichii's own compute
fallback instead (lichii-bc250 sets it itself). --native-mesh leaves it out
(remembered); EDEN_NX2_NO_MESH would skip the draws instead, which loses
geometry.

Every shortcut also gets DISABLE_VK_LAYER_VALVE_steam_fossilize_1=1 (Steam's
shader pre-cache layer records every pipeline the emulator builds and never
stops growing: 42 GB in a day on the BC-250) and DISABLE_BGFX=1 (Borderless
Gaming's Vulkan layer aborts Lichii at start; harmless where it is not
installed). Some games get their own variables on top (GAME_ENV below), for
example Zelda: Ocarina of Time its 30 fps tick and the handheld display mode,
the way lethevimlet's lichii-bc250 launcher sets them. --env wins over both.

What a run does:
  1. Games: every .nup (also .nx/.xci/.nsp) in the folder gets a shortcut
       lichii -f -g "<full path to the game file>"
     named after the game's official title. --env NAME=VALUE (repeatable) puts
     environment variables in front of it, for example
     --env EDEN_NX2_FORCE_MESH_FALLBACK=1; the choice is remembered for later
     runs and applied to every Lichii shortcut, --clear-env drops it again.
  2. Artwork and metadata, the way Playnite does it:
       - SteamGridDB when you have a (free) API key: in $SGDB_API_KEY or the
         file ~/.config/steamgriddb_api_key. Gives cover, banner, hero, logo, icon.
       - Otherwise Nintendo's own store (no key needed): the official title,
         publisher, release date, description, and its key art, cut to Steam's
         cover, banner, hero and icon sizes.
     Artwork that is already in Steam's grid folder is left alone (so art you
     picked yourself in Steam stays); --refresh-art fetches it again.
  3. Shortcuts whose game file is gone: asks per shortcut, --prune removes them
     without asking. Started from its Steam shortcut it always prunes.
  4. A game that already has a shortcut of another Lichii or Eglantine build
     gets no second one; --migrate points those shortcuts at this emulator and
     keeps their artwork, play time and Steam settings.

Steam has to be closed while its shortcut list is written: the script stops it
(only when something changed and no game is running) and starts it again. New
artwork for a shortcut Steam already shows restarts it too: a running Steam
keeps showing the shortcut without art until it starts again.

Usage: lichii-steam-sync [--dry-run] [--prune] [--refresh-art] [--migrate]
                         [--env NAME=VALUE ...] [--clear-env] [--native-mesh]
                         [--emulator lichii|lichii-bc250|PATH] [dir]
"""

